Problem #1 Report
Node Used: hostname
Compiler Version: gcc --version
Compile Flags: -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 -I…/include -O3 -lrt
Number of Repetitions: 12
Statistic Reported: bandwidth_bytes_per_s
The two data types int and double are measured, along with the two kernel versions A and B. We also have three different input orders being ‘sorted’, ‘reverse’, and ‘random’ and the three sizes being 10^6, 10^7, and 10^8. As it went through the combinations, it ran many reparations that included measurements like median time and resulting maximum value. For example, this is the output of int A and B sorted:
int A sorted n=1000000 reps=12 avg=215.385 us median=212.827 us min=212.629 us max=232.161 us rate=17711.063 MB/s ns/elem=0.215 result=131070
int B sorted n=1000000 reps=12 avg=217.059 us median=212.912 us min=212.698 us max=251.940 us rate=17574.472 MB/s ns/elem=0.217 result=131070
As I looked at each input type, they seem to all produce similar results. For the int data type, we can see that each input type only has a 1 to 2% difference compared to the double data type, where it was less than 1% of a difference. Overall, it means the input order does not affect the running time.
Based on the output, the int kernels seem to be more higher throughput (MB/s) than the double kernels. For example, 10^6 was int = 17k MB/s vs double = 7k MB/s. This could be due to the amount of bytes each input type has.
For plotting against n, the rate seems to decrease as n increases. An example is in int A’s sorted, where 10^6 is 17k MB/s, 10^7 is 10k MB/s, and 10^8 is 8k MB/s. This means that the performance may be more difficult to do as the array size grows.
When sweeping, we can see that the bandwidth stays constant for some time. As the array size increases, the bandwidth seems to decrease.
Run lscpu and record the L1, L2, and L3 cache sizes of the node. Mark them on your plot, and state how the sizes at which the bandwidth drops compare with them.
When running lscpu and looking at L1, L2, and L3, we got L1 = 1126.4 B, L2 = 36864 KB, and L3 = 51904512 B (the values are simplified in the key on the graph). The size of the array depends on when the line drops in the graph. For example, L1 has a small decrease compared to L3 showing a tremendous decrease. 
