Problem #4 Report
Node Used: hostname
Compiler Version: gcc --version
Compile Flags: -Wall -Wextra -std=c11 -I…/include -O3 -lrt
Number of Repetitions: 1
Statistic Reported: Average runtime in microseconds and lots of sorted items per second
For merge sort, we just needed to sort an array of n integers. Based upon the results, we can see that merge_reuse is the fastest in performance, while merge_per_call is the slowest in comparison with qsort. We can also see that based on the input, the sorted and equal arrays process faster than random because of such few merges. Overall, the single-allocation merge sort or merge_reuse seems to be the best in performance and scalability. Also, every method in the output is correct when sorting and preserving.
